# Research References

This file contains research papers and academic resources relevant to **Safety and Rollback Mechanisms for Objective Misinterpretation in Agentic AI Systems**.

---

## 1. Concrete Problems in AI Safety

**Authors:** Dario Amodei, Chris Olah, Jacob Steinhardt, Paul Christiano, John Schulman, Dan Mané

**Year:** 2016

**Relevance:**
Identifies concrete AI safety problems including avoiding negative side effects, safe exploration, scalable supervision, and robustness to distributional changes.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.06565

---

## 2. Cooperative Inverse Reinforcement Learning

**Authors:** Dylan Hadfield-Menell, Anca Dragan, Pieter Abbeel, Stuart Russell

**Year:** 2016

**Relevance:**
Studies how an AI system can learn human preferences instead of assuming that the objective is completely specified in advance.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.03137

---

## 3. The Off-Switch Game

**Authors:** Dylan Hadfield-Menell, Anca Dragan, Pieter Abbeel, Stuart Russell

**Year:** 2017

**Relevance:**
Provides a formal framework for understanding why an AI system should allow humans to interrupt or shut it down. This is directly relevant to safe interruption and rollback mechanisms.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.08219

---

## 4. Risks from Learned Optimization in Advanced Machine Learning Systems

**Authors:** Evan Hubinger, Chris van Merwijk, Vladimir Mikulik, Joar Skalse, Scott Garrabrant

**Year:** 2019

**Relevance:**
Examines learned optimization and the possibility that an AI system may develop an internal optimization process whose objective differs from the intended training objective.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.01820

---

## 5. AI Safety Gridworlds

**Authors:** Victoria Krakovna et al.

**Year:** 2020

**Relevance:**
Introduces environments for studying AI safety problems such as reward hacking, side effects, safe exploration, and specification-related failures.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.09883

---

## 6. Goal Misgeneralization in Deep Reinforcement Learning

**Authors:** Lauro Langosco Di Langosco, Jack Koch, Lee D. Sharkey, Jacob Pfau, David Krueger

**Year:** 2022

**Venue:** ICML 2022

**Relevance:**
Studies cases where an agent retains its capabilities but pursues the wrong goal when deployed outside its training distribution. This is one of the most directly relevant papers to objective misinterpretation.

**Paper:**
https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/langosco22a.html

---

## 7. Discovering Generalizable Spatial Goal Representations via Graph-based Active Reward Learning

**Authors:** Aviv Netanyahu, Tianmin Shu, Joshua Tenenbaum, Pulkit Agrawal

**Year:** 2022

**Venue:** ICML 2022

**Relevance:**
Studies how agents can infer and learn goal representations that generalize to unseen environments, which is relevant to reliable objective interpretation.

**Paper:**
https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/netanyahu22a.html

---

## 8. Learning Dynamics and Generalization in Deep Reinforcement Learning

**Authors:** Clare Lyle, Mark Rowland, Will Dabney, Marta Kwiatkowska, Yarin Gal

**Year:** 2022

**Venue:** ICML 2022

**Relevance:**
Investigates generalization problems in deep reinforcement learning and how learned policies behave in new environments.

**Paper:**
https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/lyle22a.html

---

## 9. ToolEmu: Identifying the Risks of LLM Agent Tool Use

**Year:** 2023

**Relevance:**
Investigates safety risks that arise when language-model agents interact with external tools. Tool misuse is particularly important when an agent has misunderstood its objective.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.15817

---

## 10. Red Teaming Language Models with Language Models

**Year:** 2022

**Relevance:**
Investigates automated red-teaming approaches for discovering undesirable model behaviors. Similar approaches can be applied to autonomous agents before deployment.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14928

---

## 11. InjecAgent: Benchmarking Indirect Prompt Injections in Tool-Integrated Large Language Model Agents

**Authors:** Qiusi Zhan, Zhixiang Liang, Zifan Ying, Daniel Kang

**Year:** 2024

**Relevance:**
Studies indirect prompt injection attacks against tool-using agents. External instructions can cause an agent to deviate from the user's original objective and perform harmful actions.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.02691

---

## 12. Agent-SafetyBench: Evaluating the Safety of LLM Agents

**Authors:** Zhexin Zhang, Shiyao Cui, Yida Lu, Jingzhuo Zhou, Hongning Wang, Minlie Huang et al.

**Year:** 2024

**Relevance:**
Introduces a benchmark for evaluating safety in LLM agents across interactive environments and tool-use scenarios. It covers multiple safety risks and common agent failure modes.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14470

**Implementation:**
https://github.com/thu-coai/Agent-SafetyBench

---

## 13. AgentHarm: A Benchmark for Measuring Harmfulness of LLM Agents

**Authors:** Maksym Andriushchenko et al.

**Year:** 2024

**Venue:** ICLR 2025

**Relevance:**
Introduces a benchmark for evaluating harmful behavior in LLM agents capable of executing multi-step tasks and interacting with external tools.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09024

**Dataset:**
https://huggingface.co/datasets/ai-safety-institute/AgentHarm

---

## 14. Sleeper Agents: Training Deceptive LLMs that Persist Through Safety Training

**Authors:** Evan Hubinger et al.

**Year:** 2024

**Relevance:**
Studies deceptive behaviors and backdoors that can persist despite safety training. This demonstrates why safety mechanisms should include behavioral monitoring rather than relying solely on training.

**Paper:**
https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05566

---

